<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Umar ibn al-Khattab</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">caliph <a href="/years/634">634</a>–644 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Umar ibn al-Khattab was the second <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a>, ruling from 634 to 644 CE. Under his leadership Muslim armies conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, and he established key institutions such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diwan">diwan</a> for paying soldiers and garrison cities. He was assassinated in 644, and accounts of the Rashidun Caliphate treat his reign as central to the caliphate's foundation and governance.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/umar-ibn-al-khattab.jpg" alt="Umar ibn al-Khattab" width="320" height="334" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: William Neuheisel from DC, US [cropped by Fazoffic] · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hagia_Sopia_6163502494_(cropped)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Umar was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, a member of the Banu Adi, a minor clan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a>, and later became its leader. Unusually among the Quraysh, he learned to read and write, and he appreciated poetry. He at first opposed Muhammad and the new faith and even set out to kill him, but on the way was turned aside by a clansman who told him that his own sister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatima">Fatima</a> bint al-Khattab and her husband had secretly become Muslims. Umar converted in 616 and then became the first Muslim to pray openly in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaaba">Kaaba</a>. He took part in nearly all of Muhammad's battles, and Muhammad gave him the title al-Fārūq, the Distinguisher.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After Muhammad's death on 8 June 632, Umar helped bring about Abu Bakr's election at the gathering at Saqifa, and he served Abu Bakr as chief adviser. He urged that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> be compiled into a single volume after the Battle of al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamama">Yamama</a>. Abu Bakr named him successor, over the objection of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talha-ibn-ubayd-allah">Talha ibn Ubayd Allah</a> that Umar was too severe. Umar was the first to take the title amir al-mu'minin, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commander-of-the-faithful">Commander of the Faithful</a>.</p><p>Under him the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> expanded across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, the Jazira and deep into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>. Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas commanded against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a> in Iraq, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-al-qadisiyyah">Battle of al-Qadisiyyah</a> led to the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amr-ibn-al-as">Amr ibn al-As</a> led the conquest of Egypt. The Sasanian state was shattered at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahavand">Nahavand</a>. Umar dismissed the successful general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalid-ibn-al-walid">Khalid ibn al-Walid</a>, reportedly to show that victory comes from God and to check a cult of personality around him.</p><p>Umar picked provincial governors for ability rather than tribal standing, and never appointed one for more than two years, so that none gathered too much local power. He set up a cabinet and formalised the Bayt al-mal, the central treasury at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>, with provincial branches. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and Christians continued under their own legal and judicial arrangements. He is credited with canal works in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a> and a canal linking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 644 Umar was assassinated by a Persian slave, Abu Lu'lu'a Firuz. His motive is unclear, though medieval sources point to a tax dispute with his master al-Mughira ibn Shu'ba. Sources differ on whether the assassin was executed or killed himself. Umar left no clear successor. Sunni Muslims revere him as a just ruler and the second of the rightly guided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a>, remembered for his plain living, while Shia Muslims view him negatively.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Umar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Abu Bakr died of illness in 634 CE and was succeeded by <b>Umar ibn al-Khattab</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=21.42,39.83&amp;year=640" aria-label="Mecca on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5620 1372 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Umar ibn al-Khattab"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6070 1642h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6070 1642h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=21.42,39.83&amp;year=640">What was here in 640 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
